```java
// JDBC Statement with string concatenation — NEVER do this
String username = request.getParameter("username"); // attacker controls this
String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '" + username + "'";

Statement stmt = connection.createStatement();
ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(sql); // database runs whatever was injected

// Attacker types:  ' OR '1'='1
// Final SQL becomes:
//   SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '' OR '1'='1'
// '1'='1' is always true -> returns every row -> auth bypass
```
*The `username` text is parsed as SQL, so the attacker rewrites the `WHERE` clause.*

```java
// Parameterized query — data and SQL travel separately
String username = request.getParameter("username");
String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?"; // ? is a placeholder

try (PreparedStatement ps = connection.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    ps.setString(1, username); // bound as a value, never as SQL
    ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery();
}

// Attacker types:  ' OR '1'='1
// The database searches for a user literally named  ' OR '1'='1
// No such user -> login fails safely. The query structure is fixed.
```
*The driver sends the query template once; the input is only ever a value, so it cannot change the query's meaning.*

```java
// String concatenation -> the input is part of the SQL text
String email = request.getParameter("email");
String sql = "SELECT id, role FROM users WHERE email = '" + email + "'";
Statement stmt = connection.createStatement();
ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery(sql);

// email = x' UNION SELECT password, 'admin' FROM users --
// The UNION leaks password hashes; -- comments out the trailing quote.
```

```java
// 1) Query template is parsed/compiled with a ? placeholder
String sql = "SELECT id, role FROM users WHERE email = ?";
try (PreparedStatement ps = connection.prepareStatement(sql)) {
    // 2) Value is bound AFTER the structure is fixed -> treated as data
    ps.setString(1, email);
    ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery();
}

// Even email = x' UNION SELECT ... is searched for as a literal string.
// The parse tree is already decided; the value cannot add a UNION.
```
*Note: the variable is bound with `setString`, never concatenated into the `sql` string.*

```java
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {

    // ANTI-PATTERN: building the query string from user input
    @Query(value = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = '" + "?#{[0]}" + "'",
           nativeQuery = true)
    List<User> findByEmailUnsafe(String email); // SpEL-built string -> injectable
}

// Worse, hand-built native SQL in a custom repo:
String sql = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE name = '" + name + "'";
entityManager.createNativeQuery(sql, User.class).getResultList(); // injectable
```
*Concatenating input into the query text bypasses every protection the ORM gives you.*

```java
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {

    // 1) Derived query — fully parameterized automatically
    Optional<User> findByEmail(String email);

    // 2) JPQL with a named parameter — bound, not concatenated
    @Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = :email")
    Optional<User> lookup(@Param("email") String email);

    // 3) Native query, still parameterized with a placeholder
    @Query(value = "SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = :email", nativeQuery = true)
    Optional<User> lookupNative(@Param("email") String email);
}
// The input is always a bound value; ' OR '1'='1 is just searched literally.
```

